You are debugging a production issue where a microservice that dynamically compiles and executes customer validation rules crashes with `java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace` every few days. The following Spring service handles script execution:

java
@Service
public class RuleEngineService {
    private final Map<String, Class<?>> ruleCache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void executeRule(String ruleId, String byteCode, DataContext context) throws Exception {
        Class<?> ruleClass = ruleCache.computeIfAbsent(ruleId, id -> {
            ByteClassLoader loader = new ByteClassLoader();
            return loader.bytesToClass(byteCode);
        });
        Rule rule = (Rule) ruleClass.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();
        rule.evaluate(context);
    }
}


When rule definitions update, `ruleId` is appended with a timestamp (e.g., `rule_123_1672531199`), generating thousands of unique keys over time.

You are debugging a production issue where an asynchronous event handling service fails to execute database updates within a transaction context. The implementation is shown below:

java
@Service
public class OrderProcessingService {

    @Autowired
    private OrderRepository orderRepository;

    @Transactional
    public void processOrder(Long orderId) {
        Order order = orderRepository.findById(orderId).orElseThrow();
        order.setStatus(OrderStatus.PROCESSING);
        orderRepository.save(order);

        CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> updateInventory(order));
    }

    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void updateInventory(Order order) {
        if (order.getQuantity() > 1000) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stock limit exceeded");
        }
        // Database update logic
    }
}


When `updateInventory` throws an `IllegalStateException`, `processOrder` commits successfully anyway, and `updateInventory` DB statements are executed outside the parent transaction.
